      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1769103214421,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "API\nGW",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "API\nGW",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Ijb3Z55yH9qtF9FAbwxQV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3550.3362965529514,
      "y": 3406.1048205232482,
      "width": 658.231950063551,
      "height": 700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0m",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1519651223,
      "version": 2680,
      "versionNonce": 937024624,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1769103214421,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "For API GW:\nTech Cosiderations:\n- Kong(Paid), TYK(Free), Krakend(Free)\n\n- For API GW I am planning to use TYK. There are multiple managed services for\nAPI GW which provides resilience, circuit breaker patterns, throttling,\nauthentication and authorization.\n\n- Not using Kong since it is a paid service and I think that this service can be\nmanaged by ourselves only, I mean TYK and Krakend also provides managed service\nwhich is a part of their paid feature so if we plan to use a manged service moving\nfrom self hosted to aa managed service will be quite easy.\n\n- Tough choice between Krakend and TYK:\n    Pros of Krakend:\n    - Very small golang binaries memory consumption is quite low for the binary itself.\n    - Provides all the features that we are also looking for.\n    Cons of Krakend:\n    - In a distributed system we cannot scale the service horizontally we have to\nscale it vertically, reason being Krakend provides an in-memory solution for all the\nabove requirements. So if I have to scale it horizontally I need a db like Redis to\nstore the data.\n\nThis con is solved by TYK since it's native integration includes Redis, it's not in-\nmemory like Krakend hence scaling it horizontally becomes quite easier.\n\nAlso the open-source version of TYK includes the Gateway and TYK Pump.\nPump is a service which is basically used for observability. It scrapes analytics data\nfrom Redis and pushes to the required analytics db that we plan to use.\n\nI am thinking of using Prometheus for analytics and Pump also provides native\nintegration for it, it also supports other analytics db like Elastic. For Prometheus\nit will expose a /metrics endpoint which it will scrape and store the data.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "For API GW:\nTech Cosiderations:\n- Kong(Paid), TYK(Free), Krakend(Free)\n\n- For API GW I am planning to use TYK. There are multiple managed services for API GW which provides resilience, circuit breaker patterns, throttling, authentication and authorization.\n\n- Not using Kong since it is a paid service and I think that this service can be managed by ourselves only, I mean TYK and Krakend also provides managed service which is a part of their paid feature so if we plan to use a manged service moving from self hosted to aa managed service will be quite easy.\n\n- Tough choice between Krakend and TYK:\n    Pros of Krakend:\n    - Very small golang binaries memory consumption is quite low for the binary itself.\n    - Provides all the features that we are also looking for.\n    Cons of Krakend:\n    - In a distributed system we cannot scale the service horizontally we have to scale it vertically, reason being Krakend provides an in-memory solution for all the above requirements. So if I have to scale it horizontally I need a db like Redis to store the data.\n\nThis con is solved by TYK since it's native integration includes Redis, it's not in-memory like Krakend hence scaling it horizontally becomes quite easier.\n\nAlso the open-source version of TYK includes the Gateway and TYK Pump.\nPump is a service which is basically used for observability. It scrapes analytics data from Redis and pushes to the required analytics db that we plan to use.\n\nI am thinking of using Prometheus for analytics and Pump also provides native integration for it, it also supports other analytics db like Elastic. For Prometheus it will expose a /metrics endpoint which it will scrape and store the data.\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
