    {
      "id": "LR7tFqMq1AeW3rgHQ4Urg",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -5922.971608227063,
      "y": 3305.6988381776187,
      "width": 735.7892528066413,
      "height": 280,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b18",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 724830105,
      "version": 1610,
      "versionNonce": 662783088,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1769103214421,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "For DB:\nI am planning to use DynamoDB. It has become by goto database recently, highly scalable\nwhen it comes to both reads and writes. Serverless and managed by AWS so I don't have to\nthink of scaling it AWS will take care of it by depending on how much scale we have we have\nwe can increase the provisioned Read/Write capacity units by default I think it provides\n1200 read and write capacity units and is pay as you go so for a small scale depending on\nthe number of requests we serve cost will be there, there is no fixed cost unlike Postgres\nAurora which is another AWS managed postgres service.\n\nAlso if I have to scale in Aurora it's a separate headache altogether. I have to pay for the\ninstance itself even though I might be serving very small scale.\n\nAnother reason I chose DynamoDB was I am planning to cache the workflows in memory if I\nam not changing it, so yeah can be cost efficient",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "For DB:\nI am planning to use DynamoDB. It has become by goto database recently, highly scalable when it comes to both reads and writes. Serverless and managed by AWS so I don't have to think of scaling it AWS will take care of it by depending on how much scale we have we have we can increase the provisioned Read/Write capacity units by default I think it provides 1200 read and write capacity units and is pay as you go so for a small scale depending on the number of requests we serve cost will be there, there is no fixed cost unlike Postgres Aurora which is another AWS managed postgres service.\n\nAlso if I have to scale in Aurora it's a separate headache altogether. I have to pay for the instance itself even though I might be serving very small scale.\n\nAnother reason I chose DynamoDB was I am planning to cache the workflows in memory if I am not changing it, so yeah can be cost efficient",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
