      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1769103214421,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Zookeeper Service",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Zookeeper Service",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "RTQgmIQ8ZwtNLPGyR21L4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -5910.762986165649,
      "y": 3867.1312305540464,
      "width": 734.7637766079106,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1B",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 415628567,
      "version": 2020,
      "versionNonce": 676878960,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1769103214421,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "For Zookeeper:\n\nOk So I am planning to use zookeeper in the following way:\n- At the startup of the Workflow Manager, all workflows will be cached in-memory, there can\nbe different kinds of workflows but it's always going to be a very small finite number I am\nassuming.\n- Each workflow id will be a zookeeper node and the value of that node will be the version of\nthat workflow. So whenever I make any new changes I will create a new workflow with the\nsame id but a different version, I will keep create a ZK Watcher library which will update the\nin-memory workflow if the version changes.\n- Whenever a new workflow is added I'll have to redeploy the service that's the catch, right\nnow I think that shouldn't be a problem but let me know what you think.\n- I will also keep a node named ZKRefreshNode which will contain timestamp\n- Whenever this timestamp changes I'll refresh the workflows keeping the existing ones and\nadd the new ones (more details of it in Injestion Service)",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "For Zookeeper:\n\nOk So I am planning to use zookeeper in the following way:\n- At the startup of the Workflow Manager, all workflows will be cached in-memory, there can be different kinds of workflows but it's always going to be a very small finite number I am assuming.\n- Each workflow id will be a zookeeper node and the value of that node will be the version of that workflow. So whenever I make any new changes I will create a new workflow with the same id but a different version, I will keep create a ZK Watcher library which will update the in-memory workflow if the version changes.\n- Whenever a new workflow is added I'll have to redeploy the service that's the catch, right now I think that shouldn't be a problem but let me know what you think.\n- I will also keep a node named ZKRefreshNode which will contain timestamp\n- Whenever this timestamp changes I'll refresh the workflows keeping the existing ones and add the new ones (more details of it in Injestion Service)",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
